{
 "id": "perfect-passive",
 "title": "Perfect Passive Indicative",
 "course": "Verbs · Passive Indicative",
 "subtitle": "Flashcards for all four conjugations plus 3rd -iō",
 "intro": "The complete passive is <b>periphrastic</b>. It's built from two words: the <b>perfect passive participle</b> (4th principal part: amātus, vīsus, missus, captus, audītus) + a form of <b>sum</b>. The participle marks the action as complete, and <b>sum marks the time</b>.<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\">Here sum is in the <b>present</b>: amātus <span class=\"end\">sum</span>, “I was loved, I have been loved.”</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\">The participle agrees with the subject in gender and number: amātus / amāta / amātum est; amātī / amātae / amāta sunt. The cards show the masculine.</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Watch out:</b> amātus sum means “I was loved,” <em>not</em> “I am loved.”</p>",
 "paradigmNote": "Participle (agrees with subject) + <span class=\"end\">sum</span> (marks time)",
